digraph ReasoningTree {
  // paper_id=s41467-025-56912-9
  rankdir="TB";
  "N1" [label="(1,0,0) Currently, 2D solids are distinguished from bulk solids because their longitudinal dimensionality is constrained."];
  "N10" [label="(12,0,0) Currently, bottom-up strategies have been developed for synthesizing 2D metal oxides."];
  "N11" [label="(13,0,0) Currently, although CVD is flexible, large-scale use is restricted by high energy intensity, limited product types, and low yield."];
  "N11X" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Given CVD faces scale restrictions and wet-chemical synthesis is attractive for cost/yield/simplicity, therefore practical general synthesis development tends to emphasize scalable low-cost chemical routes while addressing their bottlenecks."];
  "N12" [label="(14,0,0) Currently, wet-chemical synthesis offers low cost, high yield, and simple operation."];
  "N13" [label="(15,0,0) Currently, a GO-templated wet-chemical method can synthesize ultrathin 2D MOs (e.g., MgO, Al2O3, SnO2, ZrO2, TiO2, Sb2O5)."];
  "N13X" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given GO-templated wet-chemical synthesis can produce ultrathin 2D MOs and such solution syntheses are restricted by pH/solubility/hydrolysis behavior, therefore this wet-chemical route is effective but not universally general due to solution constraints."];
  "N14" [label="(17,0,0) Currently, such solution syntheses are restricted by solution pH, precursor solubility, and precursor hydrolysis behavior."];
  "N15" [label="(19,0,0) Currently, synthesizing 2D MOs remains challenging when precursors hydrolyze violently or only refractorily."];
  "N16" [label="(20,0,0) Currently, seeking a versatile and facile method to fabricate 2D metal oxide crystals is an appealing endeavor."];
  "N16X" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given 2D MOs are valuable targets and seeking a versatile/facile fabrication method is appealing, therefore developing a general synthesis route for 2D MOs is motivated."];
  "N18" [label="(21,0,0) Currently, confinement synthesis in a fixed interlayer means transverse growth of 2D guests using the layer spacing of a lamellar framework plus a template."];
  "N19" [label="(22,0,0) If the template interlayer spacing is fixed, then the longitudinal dimension (thickness) of products can be strictly limited."];
  "N2" [label="(2,0,0) Currently, many 2D materials (graphene, oxides, hydroxides, sulfides, BN) have few-atomic-layer thickness, high aspect ratio, and show interesting properties and promising applications."];
  "N20" [label="(23,0,0) Currently, solid-template interlayer confinement has enabled forming a free-standing monolayer carbon within LDH interlayers, indicating feasibility of confined synthesis using solid templates."];
  "N21" [label="(0,0,0) abduction-reasoning: Given feasibility of making monolayers via confined interlayer reactions using solid templates and the knowledge that interlayer confinement can limit thickness, therefore confinement plausibly can generally enforce 2D growth even when the target material is not intrinsically layered."];
  "N22" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given fixed interlayer confinement can strictly limit product thickness and confinement synthesis provides such fixed interlayers, therefore confinement synthesis should be able to produce ultrathin (2D/monolayer) products by restricting longitudinal growth."];
  "N2X" [label="(0,0,0) abduction-reasoning: Given 2D materials are reported as few-atomic-layer, high-aspect-ratio solids and 2D solids are defined by constrained longitudinal dimensionality, therefore the reported materials plausibly qualify as 2D because their thickness is constrained."];
  "N3" [label="(3,0,0) Currently, 2D metal oxides (2D MOs) combine redox chemistry, abundant defects, rapid mass transport, and earth abundance, giving wide applications (catalysis/energy storage/semiconductors)."];
  "N3X" [label="(0,0,0) abduction-reasoning: Given 2D materials show interesting properties/applications and 2D MOs have advantageous chemistry/defects/transport/abundance, therefore a plausible research direction is to expand accessible ultrathin 2D MO crystals."];
  "N4" [label="(4,0,0) Currently, multiple fabrication routes (exfoliation, CVD, template, solvo-/hydrothermal) have been used to make 2D architectures."];
  "N5" [label="(5,0,0) Currently, some 2D MO nanosheets have been produced via a top-down strategy analogous to graphene exfoliated from layered graphite."];
  "N5X" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Given some 2D MO nanosheets and several hexagonal MO monolayers have been produced via top-down exfoliation, therefore exfoliation can succeed for certain metal-oxide systems as demonstrated cases."];
  "N6" [label="(6,0,0) Currently, exfoliation has produced several 2D hexagonal MO monolayers (e.g., TiO2, MnO, Fe2O3)."];
  "N7" [label="(10,0,0) If a material framework is layered and interlayers are held by weak vdW forces, then exfoliation is suitable to cleave it into 2D layers."];
  "N7X" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given exfoliation is suitable for layered/vdW-held frameworks and exfoliation has succeeded for some MO systems, therefore those successful exfoliation cases likely involve layered or effectively vdW-cleavable structures and do not guarantee generality."];
  "N8" [label="(11,0,0) Currently, most metal oxides are not intrinsically layered, so forming 2D MO nanosheets via exfoliation is quite limited."];
  "N9B" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given exfoliation suitability is tied to layered/vdW frameworks and successful exfoliation cases likely reflect such frameworks, therefore exfoliation success is conditional and does not imply a general method for most metal oxides."];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This study aims to develop and evaluate a dry-chemical, melt-infiltration-assisted confined interlayer growth strategy that uses the fixed nanospaces of the lamellar host RUB-15 to convert diverse metal precursors into free-standing, highly crystalline monolayer 2D metal-oxide and sulfide nanosheets, while testing whether the products replicate the template morphology, expose defect-rich surfaces, and show enhanced catalytic performance.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) When existing 2D metal-oxide synthesis routes are limited by exfoliation generality, scaling, or solution-chemistry constraints, and fixed interlayer confinement with dry melt infiltration offers a way to restrict thickness, the paper-level aim is to develop and evaluate a more general confined-growth synthesis strategy."];
  "R16" [label="(0,0,0) Given that exfoliation is suitable for layered frameworks with weak van der Waals interlayers and that most metal oxides are not intrinsically layered, exfoliation is broadly limited for making 2D metal-oxide nanosheets and is not a general route for most MOs."];
  "R17" [label="(0,0,0) Given that GO-templated wet-chemical synthesis is effective but constrained by pH, precursor solubility, and hydrolysis behavior, and that 2D MO synthesis is challenging when precursors hydrolyze violently or only refractorily, a more universal method should reduce dependence on aqueous hydrolysis control."];
  "R4" [label="(0,0,0) Given that multiple fabrication routes are used to make 2D architectures and that bottom-up strategies have been developed for 2D metal oxides, bottom-up synthesis is one established route to consider among 2D MO fabrication options."];
  "R8" [label="(0,0,0) Given that bottom-up synthesis is an established 2D MO fabrication option and that practical synthesis development emphasizes scalable, low-cost chemical routes while addressing bottlenecks, evaluating constraints of chemical bottom-up routes is relevant to developing more general 2D MO synthesis methods."];
  "N2" -> "N2X" [label="abduction-phenomenon"];
  "N1" -> "N2X" [label="abduction-knowledge"];
  "N2X" -> "N3X" [label="abduction-phenomenon"];
  "N3" -> "N3X" [label="abduction-knowledge"];
